 ## Operating characteristics
 
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** The memory footprint is dominated by the storage of Gaussian attributes. For $N$ Gaussians, the memory scales linearly with $N$. High-order spherical harmonics (SH Degree > 1) significantly increase memory usage [S3].
-*   **Time:** The rendering time is dominated by the sorting and rasterization steps. Sorting scales with $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with radix sort, depending on the implementation [S16]. The rasterization step is $O(P \cdot K)$, where $P$ is the number of pixels and $K$ is the average number of Gaussians per pixel [S16].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   GEMM-GS achieves an average 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS implementation [S16].
-*   GEMM-GS provides an additional 1.47× speedup when combined with existing acceleration approaches [S16].
-*   LS-Gaussian achieves 5.41× speedup over the edge GPU baseline on average and up to 17.3× speedup with a customized accelerator [S8].
-
-**Scaling:**
-*   The method scales to large scenes with millions of Gaussians, but requires efficient data structures and hardware acceleration (e.g., Tensor Cores) to maintain real-time performance [S16].
-*   Distributed training and sharding allow scaling to even larger scenes by distributing the workload across multiple GPUs [S2].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering quality, explicit representation, ease of integration with existing pipelines [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, sensitivity to initialization and hyperparameters (e.g., scene extent, densification thresholds) [S4][S9, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when real-time novel view synthesis is required and the scene can be represented by a set of Gaussians [S9, unofficial].
-*   Use when hardware acceleration (CUDA, Tensor Cores) is available [S16].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Avoid when memory is severely constrained and compression methods are not sufficient [S16].
-*   Avoid when the scene has complex, non-rigid deformations that are not well-modeled by static Gaussians [S15].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The training loop is $O(N \cdot I)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $I$ is the number of iterations. Rasterization is $O(N \cdot T)$, where $T$ is the number of tiles, but optimized by spatial sorting and half-precision arithmetic [S2, S16].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot C)$, where $C$ is the size of Gaussian attributes (position, scale, rotation, opacity, SH coefficients) [S2]. For high-resolution scenes, $N$ can exceed millions, requiring significant VRAM [S16].
+*   **Measured Performance:** GEMM-GS achieves a 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS and an additional 1.47× when combined with other acceleration methods [S16]. LS-Gaussian achieves a 5.41× speedup over edge GPU baselines on Jetson AGX Orin [S8].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering speeds, high visual fidelity, and explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression [S1, S16].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, potential for "floaters" in sparse regions, and sensitivity to initialization and hyperparameters [S1, S4].
+*   **When to Use:** When real-time novel view synthesis is required and high-quality training data is available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is severely constrained and compression is not an option, or when the scene is extremely large and distributed training is not feasible [S16].
 
 ## Background
 
-**Origin:**
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. It builds on the idea of representing a scene as a set of 3D Gaussians and rendering them using differentiable rasterization.
-
-**History and Development:**
-Since its introduction, many variants and optimizations have been proposed, including methods for handling defocus blur [S5], denoising [S6], large-scale scenes [S7], and acceleration [S16]. The method has become a popular alternative to NeRF for real-time rendering.
-
-**Where it is used:**
-3D Gaussian Splatting is used in computer vision, graphics, and robotics for tasks such as novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and augmented reality [S1, S8].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. It builds on the idea of representing scenes as a collection of 3D Gaussians, which are rendered using a differentiable rasterizer. The method was developed to address the slow rendering speeds of NeRFs while maintaining high visual quality [S1]. It has since become a standard baseline for novel view synthesis and 3D reconstruction [S9, unofficial].
 
